// A real program, written to find out what is missing rather than to show off
// what is there. `vow run examples/todo.vow --dir examples`
//
// It reads a list of tasks out of a file it was handed access to, adds one if
// it was given anything on the command line, counts them, and prints the ones
// that are not done:
//
//   vow run examples/todo.vow --dir examples -- buy milk
//
// That is the smallest thing anybody would call a program, and until recently
// none of it could be written down: there was no way to hold four tasks, no way
// to turn a count into something you could print, and no way to write a file.
//
// What it found, in the order it found it:
//
// 1. Three of its functions were the same function. Each was "start at 0, look
//    at each element, stop when `at` fails", and each declared `Diverge` for
//    it, because a function that can reach itself may not return. That was the
//    argument for iteration made by a program rather than by a design
//    document, and it is what #90 decided. The three walks below are `for`
//    loops now and none of them declares anything.
//
// 2. An accumulator had to be threaded through as a parameter, because handler
//    state is the only mutable thing in the language and reaching for a
//    handler to collect four strings would be using an effect to get around
//    not having a loop. The `for` here carries one and still nothing is
//    mutable: `found` is bound again on every turn rather than assigned.
//
// 3. The file format was `x|title` because that was what the language could
//    take apart. `[x] title` needed searching or slicing and there was
//    neither, so the data was bent to fit the tool. That is fixed in #108 by
//    the one text operation that could not be written in the language, and
//    the format below is the one a person would have written first. A title
//    containing the separator still loses its tail, which was true of `|` too
//    and is a separator being a separator rather than a hole in the language.
//
// 4. Splitting on `"\n"` left a `\r` on every line of a file written on
//    Windows, and there was no way to trim one off. The first run of this
//    program printed its own output backwards over itself for exactly that
//    reason, and the repository forcing LF was a program depending on
//    repository configuration to be correct. `trim` is the reason that is no
//    longer true, and it is the better half of what #108 was for.
//
// 5. Nine of its functions were three walks and six helpers, and every one of
//    the walks was the same walk with a different body. There was no way to
//    say so. Writing `map` once rather than once per element type needed a
//    generic function (#113); writing it once rather than once per effect
//    needed a row variable (#117). Those two are what
//    `examples/list.vow` is made of, and the section below is four functions
//    now, three of them one line.
//
// 6. Pointing this file at that library found the last one. The compiler only
//    looks at the files it was handed, so `vow run examples/todo.vow` could
//    not find `examples/list` and the workaround was to name every file the
//    program needs. A library nobody can use without knowing its file layout
//    is not a library. A module's name says where it lives now (#118), which
//    is a rule every file here already followed and nothing said out loud.
//
// 7. Marking a task done, which is what this file said for months it could
//    not do, is one `map` over the tasks with one of them replaced. It took
//    generic functions, generic types and a row variable to be able to write
//    that `map` once, and once those existed the feature was four lines.
//
//    Writing it found one more thing, and a small one: `state` used to be a
//    keyword, so `with state = ..` did not parse. It means something inside a
//    `handler` and nowhere else, and reserving a word that common for one
//    position is a cost nobody had paid until somebody wanted the word. The
//    fold below is that word being wanted, so it is a name again (#128).
//
// 8. Two tasks with the same title, which this file had listed as the thing it
//    could not do. It can now: `done 2` finishes the second task and
//    `done buy milk` still finishes every task called that. Nothing in the
//    language had to change, and what it took is the interesting part.
//
//    A position is a fact about where an element is, and a callback is only
//    ever handed the element. So `map` and `filter` stopped being enough the
//    moment position mattered, and the walk went back to being written out by
//    hand with a counter in a record. There were three of those in this file,
//    all the same shape, and one of them replaced a `map(filter(..))` that
//    used to be a single line. `filter` is not imported here any more. That is
//    what item 9 below came out of.
//
//    Nothing bounds the number. `done 9` on a file with two tasks walks both,
//    marks neither and hands back the list it started with, so whether it
//    meant anything has to be asked again by a separate function that compares
//    it against the length. That is the shape an index has when the type
//    system cannot hold "in range", and it is the evidence the refinement
//    conversation was waiting for rather than a story told ahead of it.
//
//    It also cost the program a name: a task called "2" can no longer be
//    finished by title, because `done` has to decide what its argument is and
//    a `String` has nowhere to record which of the two it was. That is the
//    same mistake as using a title for a name, one level up.
//
// 9. Those three walks are one line each again. A `for` says where in the list
//    it is now, so `for task at here in tasks` binds the position and the
//    record that existed to smuggle a counter alongside the answer is gone,
//    along with the branches that only existed to keep it right.
//
//    That is the fifth thing this file decided about the language, and the
//    argument for putting it in the language rather than in the library is the
//    library itself: everything in `examples/list.vow` is written with a
//    `for`, so `map` could not hand a callback something the walk never knew.
//    `map_at` is four lines of Vow now and nothing here grew a second `map`.
module examples/todo

use examples/list.{map, any, count_where}

record Task {
    done: Bool,
    title: String,
}

// One line to a task. `?` propagates the failure of either `at`, so a line
// with no separator in it comes back as an error rather than as a task with
// half of it missing.
//
// `trim` on both halves is what lets the format be `[x] ship it` rather than
// something chosen for the parser's convenience: the mark arrives as `[x` or
// `[` with a space still on it, and the title arrives with the space that
// followed the bracket.
fn parse(line: String) -> Result<Task, String> {
    let parts = split(line, "]")
    let mark = trim(at(parts, 0)?)
    let title = trim(at(parts, 1)?)

    ok(Task { done: mark == "[x", title })
}

// The three walks, which used to be three recursive functions and three
// `Diverge` declarations. They became three `for` loops, and they are three
// calls into `examples/list.vow` now, which is a library written in Vow rather
// than built into the compiler.
//
// That is the fifth thing this file found, and it took the longest to fix.
// Every walk here was the same walk with a different body and there was no way
// to say so. Writing `map` once instead of once per element type needed a
// generic function. Writing it once instead of once per effect needed a row
// variable. The nine functions this section used to hold are four now, and
// three of them are one line.
//
// A blank line is not a task and not an error worth reporting. A file that
// ends in a newline has one, which is the sort of thing you only find out by
// running the program.
fn tasks(lines: List<String>) -> List<Task> {
    for line in lines with found = [] {
        match parse(line) {
            ok(task) => push(found, task),
            err(why) => found,
        }
    }
}

fn walk(lines: List<String>) -> Int {
    length(tasks(lines))
}

fn count_done(lines: List<String>) -> Int {
    count_where(tasks(lines), |task: Task| task.done)
}

// The open ones, numbered by where they are in the file.
//
// This used to be `map(filter(..))` and one line, then a walk carrying a
// counter in a record, and it is a `for` again because a `for` says where it
// is now. The counter has to count every task rather than every open one,
// because the number a user types has to mean the same thing here and in
// `marked_at`, and the walk that gets that right is the one that never had a
// choice about it.
//
// `here` counts from zero and the file counts from one, so the number printed
// is one more. That is a decision this file makes about what to show a person,
// which is where it belongs.
fn open_titles(lines: List<String>) -> List<String> {
    for task at here in tasks(lines) with kept = [] {
        if task.done {
            kept
        } else {
            push(kept, to_string(here + 1) + ". " + task.title)
        }
    }
}

fn report(lines: List<String>) -> String {
    to_string(count_done(lines)) + " of " + to_string(walk(lines)) + " done"
}

// Turning a task back into a line, which is `parse` run backwards. Worth
// having next to it: a program that reads a format and writes it in two
// different places is a program with two formats.
fn render(task: Task) -> String {
    if task.done {
        "[x] " + task.title
    } else {
        "[ ] " + task.title
    }
}

// The thing this file could not do for most of its life, and the reason it
// could not was never the marking. It was that there was no way to say "the
// same list with one element changed" without writing the walk out by hand,
// and there was no way to write that walk once for every element type.
//
// It is one `map` now. The library that `map` comes from is written in Vow.
fn marked(lines: List<String>, title: String) -> List<String> {
    map(tasks(lines), |task: Task| render(finish(task, title)))
}

// The same thing by position rather than by title, which is what this file
// said for its whole life it could not do.
//
// It is not one `map`. A callback is only ever handed the element, so a
// library function written in Vow cannot tell one where it is. A `for` can,
// which is why the walk is here and stays here.
//
// Nothing bounds `position`. `done 9` on a file with two tasks walks both,
// marks neither, and comes back with the list it started with, so the answer
// to "was there anything there" has to be asked separately by `holds_at`.
fn marked_at(lines: List<String>, position: Int) -> List<String> {
    for task at here in tasks(lines) with kept = [] {
        if here + 1 == position {
            push(kept, render(done(task)))
        } else {
            push(kept, render(task))
        }
    }
}

fn done(task: Task) -> Task {
    Task { done: true, title: task.title }
}

fn finish(task: Task, title: String) -> Task {
    if task.title == title {
        done(task)
    } else {
        task
    }
}

fn holds(lines: List<String>, title: String) -> Bool {
    any(tasks(lines), |task: Task| task.title == title)
}

// Whether there is a task at that position. Two comparisons rather than one
// because a position is one-based, and both of them are here rather than in
// `marked_at` because that walk has nowhere to say no.
fn holds_at(lines: List<String>, position: Int) -> Bool {
    position >= 1 && position <= walk(lines)
}

// A file that is not there is not a crash. The capability says which directory
// this may read from and nothing says what is in it.
fn read_or(files: Dir, name: String, fallback: String) -> String
  uses
    Io.read,
{
    match Io.read(files, name) {
        ok(text) => text,
        err(why) => fallback,
    }
}

// What the command line asked for. One verb and the rest is a title, so
// `-- done buy milk` finishes "buy milk" and `-- buy milk` adds it.
//
// A choice rather than a string compared three times, because the three
// answers are the whole set and a `match` on them is exhaustive. That is the
// difference between a program that handles a new case and one that forgets
// to.
//
// `done 2` finishes the second task, which is the arm this file was missing.
// It carries the cost of a program deciding what a word means: a task called
// "2" can no longer be finished by name. That is the same mistake as using a
// title for a name, one level up, and there is nowhere in the type of a
// `String` to record which of the two it is.
choice Asked {
    Nothing,
    Add { title: String },
    Finish { title: String },
    FinishAt { position: Int },
}

fn asked_for(arguments: List<String>) -> Asked {
    if length(arguments) == 0 {
        Nothing
    } else {
        match at(arguments, 0) {
            ok(first) => rest_of(first, arguments),
            err(why) => Nothing,
        }
    }
}

fn rest_of(first: String, arguments: List<String>) -> Asked {
    let title = join(after_first(arguments), " ")

    if first == "done" {
        if length(title) == 0 {
            Nothing
        } else {
            match to_int(title) {
                ok(position) => FinishAt { position },
                err(why) => Finish { title },
            }
        }
    } else {
        Add { title: join(arguments, " ") }
    }
}

// Dropping the first element. Worth writing out once here rather than putting
// it in the library: what a caller usually wants is `filter`, and a list
// library with a `drop` in it invites index arithmetic that nothing in this
// language makes safe.
//
// This was the third walk in this file carrying a counter in a record. All
// three had the same shape and the record existed to smuggle a number
// alongside the answer, which every branch then had to remember to bump. A
// `for` says where it is now, so the record is gone and so are the branches
// that only existed to keep it right.
fn after_first(items: List<String>) -> List<String> {
    for item at here in items with kept = [] {
        if here == 0 {
            kept
        } else {
            push(kept, item)
        }
    }
}

// Everything given on the command line becomes one task, so `vow run ... --
// buy milk` adds "buy milk" rather than two tasks nobody asked for. The
// trailing newline goes back on, because a text file without one is its own
// small wart and this is the only place that decides.
fn written(text: String, asked: Asked) -> String {
    match asked {
        Nothing => text,
        Add { title } => lines_of(push(entries_in(text), "[ ] " + title)),
        Finish { title } => lines_of(marked(entries_in(text), title)),
        FinishAt { position } => lines_of(marked_at(entries_in(text), position)),
    }
}

fn lines_of(entries: List<String>) -> String {
    if length(entries) == 0 {
        ""
    } else {
        join(entries, "\n") + "\n"
    }
}

// A `for` used as a filter, which is what there is: the accumulator either
// grows or does not. A file that ends in a newline has a blank entry at the
// end of it, and writing that back out would put a blank line in the middle of
// the file the next time something was added.
//
// `trim` is here for a duller reason and a more important one. A file written
// on Windows has a `\r` on the end of every line, and splitting on `"\n"`
// leaves it there. Before there was a way to take it off, this program printed
// its own output backwards over itself, and the only thing stopping that was
// the repository forcing LF. A program should not depend on how the repository
// holding it is configured.
fn entries_in(text: String) -> List<String> {
    for line in split(text, "\n") with kept = [] {
        if length(trim(line)) == 0 {
            kept
        } else {
            push(kept, trim(line))
        }
    }
}

fn main(sys: System) -> Int
  uses
    Io.write,
    Io.read,
    Io.save,
    Io.args,
{
    let out = sys.console
    let before = read_or(sys.files, "todo.txt", "")
    let asked = asked_for(Io.args(sys))
    let after = written(before, asked)

    // Saying so when a `done` named nothing that is there. Silently doing
    // nothing is how somebody finds out a week later that they misspelled it.
    Io.write(out, said(before, asked))

    // Saving only when something changed. A program that rewrote the file on
    // every run would touch it even when asked to do nothing, and a to-do list
    // whose timestamp moves for no reason is a to-do list nobody trusts.
    if before != after {
        match Io.save(sys.files, "todo.txt", after) {
            ok(nothing) => Io.write(out, "saved"),
            err(why) => Io.write(out, why),
        }
    }

    let lines = entries_in(after)

    Io.write(out, report(lines))
    Io.write(out, "still open: " + join(open_titles(lines), ", "))

    0
}

fn said(text: String, asked: Asked) -> String {
    match asked {
        Nothing => "nothing asked for",
        Add { title } => "added: " + title,
        Finish { title } => finished_or_not(text, title),
        FinishAt { position } => finished_at_or_not(text, position),
    }
}

fn finished_or_not(text: String, title: String) -> String {
    if holds(entries_in(text), title) {
        "done: " + title
    } else {
        "no task called: " + title
    }
}

fn finished_at_or_not(text: String, position: Int) -> String {
    if holds_at(entries_in(text), position) {
        "done: " + to_string(position)
    } else {
        "no task at " + to_string(position)
    }
}

test "a line becomes a task" {
    assert parse("[x] ship it") == ok(Task { done: true, title: "ship it" })
    assert parse("[ ] ship it") == ok(Task { done: false, title: "ship it" })
}

test "a line with nothing to split is not a task" {
    assert parse("") == err("index 1 is outside a list of 1")
    assert parse("no separator") == err("index 1 is outside a list of 1")
}

test "a line written on Windows is the same line" {
    assert entries_in("[x] one\r\n[ ] two\r\n") == ["[x] one", "[ ] two"]
    assert parse("[x] ship it\r") == ok(Task { done: true, title: "ship it" })
}

test "counting and collecting agree with each other" {
    let lines = ["[x] one", "[ ] two", "[ ] three", ""]

    assert walk(lines) == 3
    assert count_done(lines) == 1
    assert open_titles(lines) == ["2. two", "3. three"]
    assert report(lines) == "1 of 3 done"
}

test "an empty list is not a special case anywhere" {
    assert walk([]) == 0
    assert count_done([]) == 0
    assert open_titles([]) == []
    assert report([]) == "0 of 0 done"
}

test "marking one done leaves the others alone" {
    let lines = ["[x] one", "[ ] two", "[ ] three"]

    assert marked(lines, "two") == ["[x] one", "[x] two", "[ ] three"]
    assert marked(lines, "one") == lines
    assert marked(lines, "nope") == lines
    assert marked([], "two") == []
}

// The bug this file carried for months, and the shortest statement of it.
test "a title is not a name when two tasks share one" {
    let lines = ["[ ] buy milk", "[ ] buy milk"]

    assert marked(lines, "buy milk") == ["[x] buy milk", "[x] buy milk"]
    assert marked_at(lines, 2) == ["[ ] buy milk", "[x] buy milk"]
}

test "marking by position marks exactly one" {
    let lines = ["[x] one", "[ ] two", "[ ] three"]

    assert marked_at(lines, 2) == ["[x] one", "[x] two", "[ ] three"]
    assert marked_at(lines, 1) == lines
    assert marked_at([], 1) == []
}

// A position nothing bounds. The walk has nowhere to say no, so out of range
// is the list coming back unchanged, and whether that meant anything is a
// second question asked somewhere else.
test "a position that is not there changes nothing and is said so" {
    let lines = ["[ ] two"]

    assert marked_at(lines, 9) == lines
    assert marked_at(lines, 0) == lines
    assert marked_at(lines, 0 - 1) == lines
    assert holds_at(lines, 1) == true
    assert holds_at(lines, 9) == false
    assert holds_at(lines, 0) == false
    assert holds_at([], 1) == false
}

test "a task that is not there is said so rather than ignored" {
    let lines = ["[ ] two"]

    assert holds(lines, "two") == true
    assert holds(lines, "nope") == false
    assert holds([], "two") == false
}

test "one verb and the rest is a title" {
    assert asked_for([]) == Nothing
    assert asked_for(["buy", "milk"]) == Add { title: "buy milk" }
    assert asked_for(["done", "buy", "milk"]) == Finish { title: "buy milk" }
    // `done` on its own names nothing, so it asks for nothing rather than
    // finishing a task with an empty title.
    assert asked_for(["done"]) == Nothing
}

// What the program decided a word means, and what that decision costs.
test "done reads a number as a position" {
    assert asked_for(["done", "2"]) == FinishAt { position: 2 }
    assert asked_for(["done", "0"]) == FinishAt { position: 0 }
    // A task called "2" cannot be finished by name any more. Nothing in the
    // type of a `String` records which of the two it was meant to be.
    assert asked_for(["done", "2", "left"]) == Finish { title: "2 left" }
}

test "what gets written back" {
    let text = "[ ] two\n"

    assert written(text, Nothing) == text
    assert written(text, Add { title: "three" }) == "[ ] two\n[ ] three\n"
    assert written(text, Finish { title: "two" }) == "[x] two\n"
    assert written(text, FinishAt { position: 1 }) == "[x] two\n"
    assert written(text, FinishAt { position: 9 }) == text
    assert written("", Add { title: "first" }) == "[ ] first\n"
    assert written("", Nothing) == ""
}

test "rendering is parsing run backwards" {
    assert render(Task { done: true, title: "one" }) == "[x] one"
    assert render(Task { done: false, title: "one" }) == "[ ] one"
    assert parse(render(Task { done: true, title: "one" })) == ok(Task { done: true, title: "one" })
}
